course: language
lesson: 20
title: Is there one proper English? The big arguments
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: People have moaned that the young are "ruining English" for hundreds of years: the writer Jonathan Swift complained in 1712 and wanted rules to stop English changing; English kept changing anyway, and it is still here.
explain: Standard English is the kind taught at school and used in formal writing; dialects like "we was robbed" or "I don't want no dinner" have their own grammar, and everybody understands them, but school writing uses the standard.
explain: Many "rules" are younger than you'd think: "ain't" was used by posh people in the 1700s before grammar books attacked it, and the "never split an infinitive" rule (no word between "to" and the verb, as in Star Trek's "to boldly go") was copied from Latin in the 1800s; most modern guides say "to boldly go" is fine.
explain: English has no boss: unlike French, which has an official Académie Française, nobody in London decides what is proper; dictionaries record how people use words, they follow the language rather than control it.
explain: English belongs to everyone who speaks it: there are more people speaking it as a second language than as a first, and a Kenyan, Nigerian or Indian speaker may have spoken it all their life, so "Your English is very good!" can be a strange thing to say.
fact: In 1712 Jonathan Swift called for rules to stop English changing; people have complained about young people "ruining" English for centuries.
fact: Language changes all the time; that is not the same as getting worse.
fact: Ain't was used by all kinds of people in the 1700s, including upper-class people; in the 1800s grammar books attacked it; today it is common in speech and songs but not in formal writing.
fact: A double negative uses two negative words in one part of a sentence, like "I don't want no dinner"; it means "I don't want any dinner", not that you want dinner.
fact: Double negatives were normal in Chaucer's English in the 1300s and are normal in Ukrainian and French; standard English uses only one.
fact: "To boldly go" puts a word between "to" and the verb; some 1800s grammar writers called this wrong because Latin can't do it, but most modern style guides say it is fine.
fact: "We was" is used in many British dialects; standard English says "we were".
fact: English has no official academy; French has the Académie Française; dictionaries and style guides record how people write.
fact: In the world, more people speak English as a second language than as a first, in many local varieties.
fact: "Ask" and "aks" both come from Old English, which had both forms; Chaucer wrote "axe" for ask in the 1300s; standard English chose "ask", and "aks" lives on in some dialects.
fact: The rule "fewer for things you can count" became popular in the late 1700s; some supermarkets were reported to have changed "10 items or less" signs to "Up to 10 items" to avoid the argument.
fact: In everyday speech "there's" is often used with plurals ("there's loads of people"); careful writing uses "there are".
fact: In Nigerian English "I'm coming" can mean "I'll be back soon" when someone steps out of a room.
exercise: Mrs Briggs writes that young people are ruining English. Who said much the same in 1712? | Jonathan Swift, the writer | William Shakespeare, the playwright | Queen Victoria, in a speech | Charles Dickens, in a novel
exercise: Is English getting worse every year? | No, it changes, which isn't the same as getting worse | Yes, it loses about a hundred good words every single year | Yes, and it will stop working completely in fifty years | No, because English has never changed at all since 1712
exercise: Who used "ain't" in the 1700s? | All kinds of people, including posh ones | Only poor people, never anyone rich or important | Only children, never any grown-ups | Only sailors and pirates at sea
exercise: Rory says "I don't want no dinner." What does he mean? | He doesn't want any dinner | He does want dinner, two negatives make a positive | He wants double dinner tonight | He wants no dinner and no pudding but wants a snack
exercise: Where are double negatives normal? | In Chaucer's English, in Ukrainian and in French | Only in maths lessons, never in any real language | Only in standard written English today | Nowhere, they have never been used by anyone
exercise: Zara's app says "Never split an infinitive." What do most modern style guides say about "to boldly go"? | It's fine | It is a serious grammar error that must never be made | It is allowed only on television and in space films | It must always be changed to "boldly to go"
exercise: Where did the old rule against "to boldly go" come from? | Some 1800s writers copied it from Latin | It was in the very first English dictionary of the Romans | A law passed by Parliament in 1066 | Star Trek fans made it up as a joke
exercise: Sam's dad shouts "We was robbed!" at the football. What is standard English? | We were robbed | We is robbed | We be robbed | Us was robbed
exercise: Who decides what is proper English? | Nobody officially; it's decided by how people use it | A special office in London with an English boss | The King, who signs a list of allowed words each year | The BBC, which has the power to ban words
exercise: Which language has an official academy that advises on its words? | French | English | Australian | Scottish English
exercise: Ava's Kenyan friend speaks English as her first language. A man says "Your English is very good!" Why is that odd? | Many Kenyans speak English fluently from childhood | Kenya is in Europe, so everyone there speaks English | English is the only language spoken in Africa | Nobody in Kenya has ever spoken English before
exercise: Lucy hears "aks" called wrong. What is true? | It's an old form; Chaucer wrote "axe" for ask | It's a new mistake that started in the last few years | It was invented by a TV programme in 2005 | It is a spelling mistake from texting
exercise: Tom's grandad shouts at a "Ten items or less" sign. What did some supermarkets reportedly do? | Changed signs to "Up to 10 items" | Changed signs to "Ten items or fewer, or else" | Took away all their signs and their tills | Let customers buy only ten items a year
exercise: Mum says "There's loads of people here." What would careful writing say? | There are loads of people here | There is loads of peoples here | Their are loads of people here | There's are loads of people here
exercise: A Lagos boy says "I'm coming" as he leaves the room. What does he mean? | I'll be back soon | I'm arriving right now | I'm leaving forever | Come with me now
exercise: Which is the best way to think about standard English and dialects? | Standard English is the school form, and dialects have their own grammar too | Standard English is the only real English and dialects are mistakes | Dialects are the only real English and standard English is fake | Both are wrong, and nobody should speak either of them
test: Your nan goes "kids today are ruining English, it was perfect in my day." What's the fair answer? | People have said that for centuries; English changes but keeps working | She's right, English was perfect in her day and will soon stop working | She's right, and English was even more perfect in 1066 | English has never changed, so it can't be ruined
test: Your dad says "Two negatives make a positive, so 'I ain't got no money' means you've got money!" Is he right? | No, everyone understands it means "I have no money" | Yes, it means he has lots of money hidden somewhere | Yes, because language works exactly like maths | No, it means he has exactly two pounds
test: A Gen Z cousin says "ain't is lowkey a poor people's word, always was." ("Lowkey" means kind of.) What's the fact? | Posh people used it too in the 1700s | She's right, rich people never used it | It was invented by rappers in the 1990s | It was only ever used in America
test: What would happen if English had an official academy that banned new words? | People would probably keep using new words anyway | Nobody would ever say a new word again, not even once | English would turn into French overnight | All the old words would be banned too
test: A teacher says "In Ukrainian you say 'я нічого не знаю' (I nothing not know)." What does that show? | Double negatives are normal in some languages | Ukrainian grammar is broken and wrong, like a mistake | Ukrainians mean they do know everything | It's a mistake that only children make
test: A Sheffield dad says "We was robbed, that weren't a penalty!" ("Weren't" here means wasn't.) Is he stupid? | No, he's using his dialect, which everyone understands | Yes, he doesn't know any grammar at all | Yes, Sheffield people can't speak English | No, he's speaking French by mistake
test: In a cartoon, a grammar robot says "To boldly go is ILLEGAL!" and explodes. What's the truth? | Most modern style guides say "to boldly go" is fine | It really is illegal in Britain to split an infinitive | It's illegal only in space films | The robot is right, Star Trek was banned
test: A man on a phone-in says "speak properly, like they decide in London!" Who actually decides? | Nobody officially; dictionaries follow how people use words | A group of judges in London who meet every Friday | The King and the Prime Minister together | The teachers of one school in Oxford
test: Your Nigerian mate says "I'm coming" and walks out. Your nan thinks he's rude. What's going on? | In Nigerian English it can mean "I'll be back soon" | He's being rude and never coming back | He's lost and doesn't know where he's going | He has misheard and thinks someone called him
test: Puzzle: "I never said nothing to nobody." How many negative words are in it? | Three: never, nothing, nobody | Two: never and nothing | One: never | Four: I, never, nothing, nobody
test: Puzzle: in standard English one negative is enough. Which sentence means the same as "I don't want no dinner" in standard English? | I don't want any dinner | I do want some dinner | I want no dinner and some | I don't not want dinner
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of change in the English language? | Continuous alteration, which does not in itself constitute deterioration | A steady decline toward eventual unintelligibility within a century | A complete cessation of change since 1712 | Alteration directed annually by royal command
exam: Identify the meaning ordinarily conveyed by the utterance "I don't want no dinner". | The speaker does not wish to have dinner | The speaker wishes to have dinner | The speaker wishes to have two dinners | The utterance conveys no meaning whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the regulation of the English language. | No official body governs English; dictionaries record usage | An academy in London determines all permissible words | Parliament publishes an annual list of approved words | The BBC is empowered by statute to prohibit words
exam: In respect of the expression "to boldly go", which statement is most accurate? | Most modern authorities regard it as acceptable | It is universally condemned as a grave error | It is prohibited by law in the United Kingdom | It is acceptable in Latin but not in English
